 you have those days where  you come in and the room feels really  tense or it feels really heavy or  there's just not the energy or the level  that you need going on you know and  you have to figure out what are those  motivators for the people that are  working there today you know  is it they just need to kind of let  loose and get some stress off and have a  little fun is that that they need a bit  of a kick in the ass and and need to  kind of get going and have someone push  them forward to wake up a little bit  like  and so i think i didn't really pay as  much attention to that in the beginning  as i probably should have and i think  it's something i'm really conscious of  now and will continue to be  as they move forward kind of growing the  business yeah it's really interesting  it's yeah like a balance of  have setting and having really high  standards  along with being mindful of the sort of  long-term culture that you want to  create  where people enjoy working there they  don't feel like just getting their balls  busted all the time like it's yeah  but also having high standards and  people knowing what they need if they  turn up today they're under the fade and  logo what's expected of them  right yeah that's interesting yeah and  then have you  and then have you only hired typically  chinese out of interest  no i have hired some experienced people  um and i have had a couple people  transfer  internally with some experience in the  business oh really okay  so just interesting point uh before we  finish you know  you said around i would have been a bit  more mindful around the local talent  that's actually something that has been  a bit of a learning when speaking to uk  businesses going into the u.s yeah and i  think sometimes it can be underestimated  i think the remote pieces obviously had  a positive impact on this or affected it  but  i spoke with quite a few uk recruiters  that then launched american parts of the  the business where i don't know i